## ABSTRACT
Li-S batteries (LSBs) have been considering as new and promising energy storage systems because of the high theoretical energy density and low price. Nevertheless, their practical application is inhibited by several factors, including poor electrical conductivity of electrode materials, greatly volumetric variation, as well as the polysulfide formation upon the cycling. To address these problems, it is imperative to develop and design effective and suitable sulfur host anode materials. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs)-based cathode materials, possessing their good conductivity and easy morphology design, have been extensively studied and exhibited enormously potential in LSBs. In this review, a comprehensive overview of MOFs-based sulfur host materials is provided, including their electrochemical reaction mechanisms, related evaluation parameters, and their performances used in LSBs in the past few years. In particular, the recent advances using in-situ characterization technologies for investigating the electrochemical reaction mechanism in LSBs are presented and highlighted. Additionally, the challenges and prospects associated with future research on MOF-related sulfur host materials are discussed. It is anticipated to offer the guidance for the identification of suitable MOFs-based sulfur cathode materials for high-performance LSBs, thereby contributing for the achievement of a sustainable and renewable society.
## KEYWORDS
lithium metal anode, sulfur cathode, metal organic frameworks, host, energy density, in-situ characterization
## 1 Introduction
Over the past two decades, renewable energy storage devices have gained significant attention as a mean to address the pollution of the environment and the impending energy crisis [1, 2]. Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), a well-established and dependable rechargeable battery system, have dominated the energy storage market since their commercialization in the late 1990s [3, 4]. However, their energy density has plateaued at around 300 Wh·kg\(^{-1}\) [5, 6], limiting their ability to satisfy the ever-increasing demand for lower cost and higher energy density. Hence, the development of the next generation energy storage device with low price and high energy density is highly desirable and imperative.
Li-S batteries (LSBs), with the high theoretical specific capacity \((1672\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and energy density \((2600\;\mathrm{Wh}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})\), are considered as one of the most potential next-generation energy storage devices to meet future needs and have attracted significant attention. More significantly, the plenitude of S on the Earth's crust, its low price, and nontoxic make LSBs cost-effective and commercially competitive with other batteries, especially traditional LIBs (Fig. 1(a)), thus vastly meeting the developing demand of renewable and clean energy [7, 8]. Despite these advantages, the application of high-performance LSBs is still impeded by some major issues, leading to unacceptable practical capacity and cycling stability. Firstly, sulfur's poor electrical conductivity \((5\ \times\ 10^{-30}\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\) at \(25\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and those of the discharging products (lithium sulfide \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2})\) significantly increase some internal resistance of the batteries, limiting the utilization efficiency of the active materials (Fig. 1(b)). The second issue is the severe volumetric expansion of sulfur cathodes (up to \(80\%\) ) after the lithiation process, causing the structural pulverization of electrodes and serious capacity fade and safety concerns. Finally, the soluble polysulfides (PSs) produced in the cathode during the charging/discharging process may easily pass across the separator and react with the Li anode to generate insoluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\). This phenomenon, known as the "shuttle effect", brings about low utilization of active materials and Coulombic efficiency (CE), low cyclic stability, and potential safety issues [9–13].
