In order to address the challenges associated with soluble polysulfide shuttle in LSBs, significant efforts have been made to develop advanced sulfur cathodes. The core–shell sulfur composites demonstrate numerous advantages when applied for the sulfur cathode. However, despite the feasibility of producing these structured composites via infiltration strategies such as dissolution–diffusion, melt–diffusion, or vapor-phase infusion, their overall performance remains limited due to conflicting requirements involving both easy infiltration and tight sealing [4, 5]. In contrast, the encapsulation of sulfur has emerged as an alternative strategy that has attracted broad interest. To date, several encapsulation strategies have been explored to further address the challenges associated with the polysulfide dissolution and shuttle effect in LSBs [6–9]. Typically, the encapsulation strategies can be categorized according to the used shell material, for instance, polymer types such as polypyridine (PPY) are utilized via an in-situ chemical oxidation polymerization process. Metal types like Ga are employed by mixing sulfur particles with host materials. Sulfide types like \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) are utilized through a self-assembly process involving nanoflakes. Moreover, oxide types are employed in hydrolysis or rebox reaction processes on the surface of sulfur particles. Notably, when metal sulfides are chosen as the sulfur precursor to be encapsulated, the volume fluctuation should be taken into account. This involves potential volume expansion that may lead to pulverization of the carbon shell and subsequent volume compression, consequently affecting the energy density. Lastly, the integration of the infiltration–encapsulation strategies offers a superior approach, providing a promise for large-scale production of encapsulated sulfur particles [10, 14].
An ideal sulfur cathode should possess features such as sufficient porosity, high electrical conductivity, and effective surface chemical absorption capabilities toward PSs. Among them, porosity is regarded as the most important one. In this case, tremendous materials have been developed as hosts for sulfur. Notably, carbonaceous hollow structures such as hollow carbon spheres and carbon nanotubes (CNTs) represent typical examples. Beyond these, polymers, metal chalcogenides (MCs), metal nitrides/hydroxides, and their carbon composites have also been extensively engineered into hollow structures to serve as hosts for sulfur [15, 16]. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are typically made up of metal ion/cluster (e.g., transition metals such as zinc copper, iron, cobalt, and nickel, alkaline earth metals such as strontium and barium, p-block elements such as gallium and indium, lanthanides such as lanthanum and cerium, and actinides such as uranium and thorium) and organic ligands (amides, pyridyl, carboxylates, polyamines, imidazole, etc.) through the interactions including strong metal–ligand bonding, H-bond, \(\pi{-}\pi\) bond, van der Waals interaction, and so on [4, 17–19]. In stark contrast, MOFs with the unique advantages including high porosity of more than \(0.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), extremely high specific surface areas of no less than \(3000~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), adjustable pore sizes, and controllable pore structure have been considered to be the most potential sulfur host materials to enhance the capacity and cyclic stability of LSBs. As illustrated in Fig. 1(c), the growing interest in MOFs for sulfur cathodes has been demonstrated by the increasing number of the publications from 2013 to 2023. On the one hand, the high surface area of MOFs offers numerous active sites for strong interactions, which effectively prevent the diffusion of PSs. Additionally, high porosity is beneficial for high sulfur loading, and optimizing the pore size can confine soluble PSs. Controllable pore structure allows for fast penetration of the electrolyte and ions/electrons diffusion [20, 21]. Despite these desirable advantages, the poor electrical conductivity and bad electrochemical stability of original MOFs have limited the application of them in LSBs. However, MOFs could be combined with the conductive material including graphene, carbon nanotube, as well as some polymers with high conductivity to improve electrical conductivity and electrochemical reactivity. Recent studies have demonstrated that pristine MOFs can be employed as versatile precursors or templates to fabricate various MOF derivative, including carbonaceous material, metal compound, and their composite. Also, MOFs composites and derivatives have shown great potential in energy storage, particularly as the sulfur cathodes for Li-S batteries [22–24]. Consequently, the electrochemical performance of S cathodes in LSBs could be further improved with MOF composites and derivatives, opening new avenues for rationally designing the advanced S cathode materials (Fig. 1(d)).
