or templates to fabricate various MOF derivative, including carbonaceous material, metal compound, and their composite. Also, MOFs composites and derivatives have shown great potential in energy storage, particularly as the sulfur cathodes for Li-S batteries [22–24]. Consequently, the electrochemical performance of S cathodes in LSBs could be further improved with MOF composites and derivatives, opening new avenues for rationally designing the advanced S cathode materials (Fig. 1(d)).
Herein, a comprehensive overview of MOFs-related materials, containing pristine MOFs, MOFs composite, MOFs derivative, and MOFs derivative composite as sulfur cathode materials in LSBs, is provided. Especially, the working principle and history of LSBs, the key factors for evaluating their performance, and in-situ characterizations of MOFs-related materials in LSBs (Figs. 1(e) and 1(f)) are systematically introduced. Finally, the urgent challenges and prospects for further rationally developing the MOFs-related materials as advanced sulfur cathode materials in LSBs are summarized. It expected that this review will guide the developing and design of suitable MOFs-related sulfur cathode materials for high-performance LSBs and inspire future advancements in the field to achieve a sustainable and renewable society.
## 2 Fundamentals and evaluation parameters of Li-S batteries
The proof-of-concept for LSBs could be dated back to 1962 when sulfur was firstly utilized as a cathode material, and the electrochemical reaction of \(2\mathrm{Li}+\mathrm{S}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) was proposed by Herbert and Ulam (Fig. 2) [25]. However, early research on LSBs was limited by the insulation of S and the very serious dissolution of lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) in organic electrolytes, leading to issues including short cycling stability and poor sulfur utilization [55, 56]. Consequently, research on Li-S batteries almost came to a halt, especially as the commercialization of Li-ion batteries (LIBs)
by Sony in 1991 [57]. In 2009, Nazar and colleagues achieved a breakthrough by using CMK-3, a highly ordered mesopore carbon material, as a S based cathode to improve cycle performance through the encapsulation of molten S into the mesopores of CMK-3 [58]. Henceforward, LSBs have attained widespread attentions and developed rapidly.
A Li-S battery is a storing energy equipment that converts chemical energy to electrical energy through electrochemical reactions between S cathode and Li metal anode. A representative Li-S battery, similar to the LIBs, comprises a cathode, anode, separator, and electrolyte, as depicted in Fig. 1(f). The cathode is composed of S as the active material, conductive additive, and a binder, which is usually coated onto the current collector. Here, the current collector is utilized to maintain electron flow during charge and discharge cycling and ensure a uniform distribution of charge across electrode layers. Al foil is the most commonly used current collector in Li-S batteries. Other types of current collectors such as graphene, carbon cloth (CC), carbon paper, and nickel foam have also been studied in some cases [13]. Since the electronic conductivity of pure sulfur is very poor, as low as \(5\,\times\,10^{-30}\,\,\mathrm{S}\mathrm{{\cdot}c m}^{-1}\) at \(25~^{\circ}\!C.\) . Therefore, incorporating conductive additives is necessary to ensure the smooth transfer of electrons to sulfur. The most common conductive additives are porous or conductive carbon, including carbon black (Super P, Ketjen Black), acetylene black, ethylene carbonate, and aligned carbon nanotubes (A-CNT). Binders are typically polymeric materials that bind conductive additive and active sulfur together.
Additionally, binders act as a significant role during the charge/discharge cycling process, ensuring the structural and morphological integrity of electrodes. Some binders, including carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC), polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF), and alginate, are usually employed in LSBs [59]. As for the anode material, considering the charming energy density, the metallic Li is regarded as the most commonly used one. Besides, the conventional separator used in Li-S batteries is a commercial Celgard polypropylene (PP) separator, which is porous and allows for continuous \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ion transport between cathode and anode. Different types of separators, such as glass fiber, polymer, and other insulating materials have also been used in LSBs [13, 59]. The electrolyte serves as the medium for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ion transportation between electrodes. Differing from the cheap \(\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\) salt applied in commercial LIBs, the electrolyte employed in LSBs always consists of 1 M lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) dissolved into a mixture of 1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME) and 1,3-dialoxane (DOL) contains 1 wt.% of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\).
## 2.1 Fundamentals and challenges of Li-S batteries
A typical LSB is configured by the S based cathode with highcapacity and light-weight Li anode, in which a multi-electron and reversible redox reaction occurs. A charging/discharging voltage profile of the LSB is typically displayed as shown in Fig. 1(b), which commonly exhibits two obvious voltage plateaus in etherbased electrolytes. These two voltage plateaus contribute to \(25\%\) and \(75\%\) of the total theoretical capacity, respectively, equivalent to 418 and \(1255\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) [12, 60–62]. During discharge or lithiation, metallic Li is oxidized into Li ions and electrons \(\left(\mathrm{e^{-}}\right)\) in the anode. \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ion and electron pass across the electrolyte and the external circuitry separately to move to the cathode, which thus generates the electrical current. In the cathode, cyclic octa sulfur \(\left(\mathsf{S}_{8}\right)\) , the most stable and common form among more than 30 solid allotropes of sulfur [13], accepts \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions and electrons, and then reacts with them to form a range of soluble LiPSs \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n},\,3\leq n\leq8)\) and eventually insoluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) which involves complex compositional and structural evolutions as well as phase changes of sulfur species. During subsequent charging or de-lithiation, lithium sulfide is reversibly reconverted into elemental sulfur, which undergoes the formation of polysulfide intermediates, leading to the reversible cycling. The electrochemical discharge/charge process at the anode and cathode could be described in the following reactions [55, 61, 63].
## 2.1.1 Discharge
Anode: Lithium metal (Li) loses electrons \(\left(\mathrm{e^{-}}\right)\) and is converted into \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions
\[
16\mathrm{Li}\rightarrow16\mathrm{Li}^{+}+16\mathrm{e}^{-}
\]
Cathode: Cyclic octa sulfur is converted into \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) through a 16-electron reducing reaction [56]
\[
\mathrm{S}_8+16\mathrm{Li}^++16\mathrm{e}^+\rightarrow8\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}
\]
The elemental sulfur reduction is typically classified into four stages, involving the changes of composition, structure, phase, and voltage–capacity profile.
Stage I: At about \(2.4~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) ), solid sulfur \(\left(\mathsf{S}_{8}\right)\) accepts \(2\mathrm{e^{-}}\) and is electrochemically reduced, and subsequently, reacts with lithium ion moving from the anode to produce soluble and longchain \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8},\) which is a solid–liquid transition reaction
\[
\mathrm{S}_8+2\mathrm{Li}^++2\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_8
\]
Stage II: At about \(2.4–2.2\mathrm{~V~}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) ), the soluble and longchain \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) formed on anode accept two electrons \(\left(\mathrm{e^{-}}\right)\) and are
further reduced to the soluble and short-chain \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4},\) which is a liquid–liquid transition reaction, where \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) could be converted to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\)
\[
3S_{8}^{2-}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow4S_{6}^{2-}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_8+2\mathrm{Li}^++2\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow2\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_4
\]
\[
2S_{6}^{2-}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow3S_{4}^{2-}
\]
Stage III (liquid–solid transition process): At roughly \(2.2~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) ), the soluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) further reacts with \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions to form solid \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) which is a liquid–solid transition reaction. In addition, this stage maintains a long plateau (Fig. 1(b))
\[
\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_4+2\mathrm{Li}^++2\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow2\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_2
\]
\[
\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_4+6\mathrm{Li}^++6\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow4\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}
\]
Stage IV (solid–solid transition process): Below about \(2.15~\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) ), soluble solid \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) is ultimately converted into insoluble and nonconductive solid \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) which is a solid–solid transition reaction
\[
\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}_2+2\mathrm{Li}^++2\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow2\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}
\]
## 2.1.2 Charge
Anode: \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions accept electrons \(\left(\mathrm{e^{-}}\right)\) to produce Li metal, which is the opposite reaction of the discharge
\[
16\mathrm{Li}^{+}+16\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow16\mathrm{Li}
\]
Cathode: \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) loses electrons \(\left(\mathrm{e^{-}}\right)\) and is reversibly converted to the \(\mathsf{S}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions, which presents a single charge platform at about \(2.4\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}\) )
\[
8\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}\rightarrow\mathrm{S}_8+16\mathrm{Li}^{+}+16\mathrm{e}^{+}
\]
The entire reaction equation is shown as follows
\[
\mathrm{S}+2\mathrm{Li}=\mathrm{Li}_2\mathrm{S}
\]
Overall, Li-S batteries involve inherent redox electrochemistry between anode and cathode during the solid–liquid–solid transition. However, several technical challenges still impede the practical applications of LSBs, which will be discussed in detail below.
