## 2.1.4 High solubility of LiPSs \(\left(L i_{2}S_{4}\) to \(L i_{2}S_{8}\right)\)
Within the charging/discharging cycling procedure, LiPSs intermediates could dissolve readily into the ether-based organic electrolyte. Ideally, these soluble products are, to a certain extent, beneficial to improve the sulfur utilization to meet practical demands of high-energy-density and enhance the kinetic behaviors through offering decent contact with conductive host. Nevertheless, the case is that dissolution of LiPSs causes more issues [55]. For instance, some soluble polysulfide could migrate from the sulfur cathode to the Li anode under the concentration gradient force. In the anode, they can be chemically reduced into insoluble as well as short-chain LiPSs and deposit onto the Li metal anode surface, leading to the irreversible loss of active materials and the passivation of lithium metal anode. Moreover, the short-chain LiPSs deposited onto the Li metal anode can partially move back to the sulfur cathode driven by the electric field and be re-oxidized to form long-chain LiPSs. This back-and-forth motion of LiPSs between the lithium metal anode and the sulfur cathode is called as the "shuttle effect", which is considered one of the most critical restrictions for Li-S batteries [56,62,65]. Especially, the parasitic shuttling effect can induce unsatisfactory cycle life, low CE, and serious self-discharging of LSBs [66].
## 2.1.5 Large volumetric change of sulfur
The crystal density of \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) is \(2.07~\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\), which is higher than \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) with the density of \(1.66~\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) [60,62]. Due to the difference in density, sulfur undergoes a significant volumetric expansion/shrink upon the charging/discharging cycle, which is about \(80\%\) sometimes, causing the pulverization and structural damage of cathode active materials and the disassembling of sulfur from the conductive current collector. The isolated sulfur is difficult to reuse in the subsequent electrochemical reaction because of the loss of electric contact, which can result in poor utilization of the active sulfur and irreversible and permanent capacity decay. Therefore, it is necessary to provide suitable void spaces to adapt the volumetric variation of active materials and maintain the structurally-complete of the sulfur cathode, which will cause a decrease in volumetric energy density [62]. Thus, the porosity of the electrode would be carefully controlled to reach the balance between performance and volumetric energy density.
## 2.1.6 The utilization of Li metal anode
Li metal is an ideal anode material for high-energy-density batteries because of the ultrahigh reversible capacity of \(3860~\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and ultralow reducing potential of \(-3.04\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. the standard hydrogen electrode [62]. However, it is well-known that Li metal is unstable and highly reactive, which poses a critical challenge for developing the stable LSBs. The active metallic lithium could spontaneously react with the organic electrolytes, resulting in electrolyte decomposition and the forming of an unstable and inhomogeneous solid electrolyte interface (SEI) on the lithium metal anode surface. The large volume changes caused by the repeated lithium plating and stripping during charge/discharge cycling result in continuous irreversible consumption of both lithium metal and electrolytes, leading to a
loss of CE [56,64]. Moreover, non-uniform current distribution on the sulfur of the Li metal anode and lithium ions concentration gradients in the electrode/electrolyte interface can induce dendrite growth of lithium metal upon the cycle, potentially leading to severe security concern because of the possibility for interior short circuits, particularly at high current densities [56,66]. In general, it is necessary to design and construct suitable materials in LSBs to address these issues effectively.
## 2.2 Evaluation parameters of Li-S batteries
The ultimate goal of LSBs is to realize cost-effective, high-performance, and long-lasting energy storage devices. This section will provide a detailed analysis of several parameters that are crucial for researching Li-S batteries. These parameters include gravimetric and volumetric energy densities, power density, compaction density, sulfur content and loading, electrolyte-to-sulfur ratio, electrolyte-to-capacity ratio, negative-to-positive capacity ratio, potential window, cycling life, safety, and economic cost.
## 2.2.1 Energy density
Energy density is one of the most critical parameters for evaluating the performance of LSBs. Gravimetric energy density represents energy per unit weight, shown as \(\mathrm{Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\), while volumetric energy density stands for energy per unit volume, expressed as \(\mathrm{Wh{\cdot}L^{-1}}\). These parameters are directly related to the intrinsic property of the battery's anode and cathode. LSBs are anticipated to provide a gravimetric energy density of \(700{-}600~\mathrm{Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\), with the volume energy density of up to \(700~\mathrm{\:Wh{\cdot}L^{-1}}\) for a fully packaged system [67,68].
The gravimetric energy density could be determined relying on Eq. (13)
\[
E=\frac{Q\times V}{m_{\mathrm{c}}+m_{\mathrm{a}}}
\]
where \(Q\) is the charging capacity delivered upon electrochemical reaction, \(V\) is the average cell operating voltage, relying on the redox potential difference between anode and cathode, and \(m_{\mathrm{a}}\) and \(m_{\mathrm{c}}\) are the weight of anode and cathode, respectively. Additionally, the charging capacity can be computed according to the reversible capacity and the mass of electrode, and simultaneously the value of the charge capacity delivered at cathode is equal to that of the charging capacity delivered at anode. Therefore, Eq. (14) can be obtained
\[
Q=C_{\mathrm{c}}\times m_{\mathrm{c}}=C_{\mathrm{a}}\times m_{\mathrm{a}}
\]
where \(C_{\mathrm{a}}\) and \(C_{\mathrm{c}}\) stand for the reversible capacity of anode and cathode, respectively. Equation (15) can be derived through combining Eqs. (13) and (14), shown as the following
\[
E=\frac{C_{\mathrm{c}}\times C_{\mathrm{a}}\times V}{C_{\mathrm{c}}+C_{\mathrm{a}}}
\]
It is evident that energy density of the Li battery is closely related to the reversible capacity and redox potentials of anode and cathode. Volumetric energy density could be calculated in a similar manner by replacing weight-specific capacity with volumetric specific capacity. Therefore, a large difference between the redox potentials of anode and cathode, as well as high specific capacities of both, is crucial for achieving high energy density [69,70].
Although energy density is typically measured according to the weight of the electrode, practical application must also take into account the additional mass of other components, including electrolytes, separators, and packaging. In this context, electrodes
with poor loading of active material might provide a good specific capacity based on Eq. (15). However, they often result in a low practical energy density due to the inactive components with a comparatively high proportion. Hence, in addition to reversible capacity and potentials of anode and cathode, it is essential to design electrode with a high loading mass of active material to achieve high energy density [29, 69].
## 2.2.2 Power density
Power density refers to the rate at which a unit weight battery can output energy during discharging. It is a measure of rate performance, which describes how much current the battery can discharge while working at a large current [69]. Power density is associated with the intrinsic properties of the materials in the battery, as is energy density. There is no direct relationship between energy density and power density. Higher energy density does not necessarily correspond to higher power density. Energy density determines the driving range whereas power density determines the acceleration speed in electric vehicles. However, high power density, in correspondence with fast energy storage, usually causes a decrease in energy density because of slow reaction kinetic at high charging/discharging rates, reducing the charge capacity and output voltage of the battery [71].
It is essential to note that both power density and energy density are variable quantities. After numerous cycles of use, the battery's power density and energy density may decline as the capacity of the battery decreases. Additionally, they also change with environmental factors. Energy storage devices with the combination of high-power density and high energy density are increasingly in demand. One possible way is to design and develop materials which could keep large amounts of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions and a special electrode structure that can provide adequate paths for the fast transfer of lithium ion and electron within a short duration of charge/discharge [72].
## 2.2.3 Compaction density
In the process of lithium battery engineering, the significance of compaction density must be emphasized as it has a considerable impact on the battery performance. Experimental evidence has shown that different compaction densities have direct effect on the thicknesses of the electrodes and the absorption capacity of the electrolyte, which subsequently affects the specific energy density, internal resistance, and cycle stability of the battery [73]. In general, thinner electrode is related to higher compactness, which can affect the volume of the battery to a certain extent. From this perspective, raising the compaction density is an effective method to improve the volumetric specific energy of Li-S batteries. From a microscopic viewpoint, tighter contact between particles and reduced particle distances can ensure the required electrical contact, promote the effective electron transport, lower the impedance, and improve the performance [68, 73]. Moreover, electrolyte in the liquid state will penetrate the gaps between particles until reaching a saturated state when sufficient electrolyte is injected. If the contact between particles is tighter, the corresponding absorption capacity of the electrolyte will decrease significantly, leading to an increase in specific energy density. Higher compaction density generally corresponds to higher battery capacity. However, if the compaction density is too high, the electrolyte's wettability can dominate, leading to reduced electronic conductivity. Thus, it is essential to use a small amount of electrolyte with the desirable wettability. For example, Hagen et al. [74] observed that the compaction density of sulfur electrodes reported in numerous literatures typically spans between 0.2 and \(0.5\ \mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\). Li et al. recently demonstrated that the \(\mathrm{S/C}\) cathode with a moderate compaction density of \(1.2\ \mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) exhibits
enhanced structural stability, superior electrolyte wettability, improved interfacial compatibility, and excellent polysulfides restraining ability compared to \(\mathrm{S/C}\) cathodes with \(1.4\ \mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) [75–77]. Additionally, the issue of volumetric change requires suitable void spaces to alleviate volumetric expansion and maintain structural integrity during the charge/discharge, which can unavoidably affect the specific capacity [73]. In summary, it is necessary to control the compaction density of electrodes to achieve the balance between specific energy density and electrochemical performance.
## 2.2.4 Sulfur content and sulfur loading
Both sulfur content \((\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) and sulfur loading \((\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) are also crucial parameters in the evaluation of Li-S batteries. The sulfur content means the weight percentage of sulfur in the active material whereas the sulfur loading refers to the areal sulfur loading of prepared electrode. These parameters are typically associated with sulfur utilization \((\mathrm{wt.}\%)\), particularly at high current density. Thence, it is essential for improving sulfur loading and sulfur content, as well as sulfur utilization, as much as possible. The area sulfur loading of prepared electrodes usually decreases with the decrease in sulfur content and the increase in sulfur utilization. Notably, recent publications pay significant attention to these indicators. Furthermore, sulfur content and sulfur utilization jointly determine the reversible capacity of LSBs. High sulfur content and high sulfur utilization could result in high reversible capacity. While, high sulfur content often coincides with low sulfur utilization owing to the low conductivity of sulfur. This leads to sluggish kinetic for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ion and electron. Consequently, it is crucial to achieve a balance between sulfur utilization and sulfur content for obtaining a higher specific capacity.
It is also worthy of remark that the specific areal capacity relies on both areal sulfur loading and sulfur utilization. The detailed relationship between them is expressed as Eqs. (16) and (17) [73, 78, 79]
Areal capacity \((\mathrm{mAh}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})=\) TSC \((\mathrm{mAh}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})\times\mathrm{S}\) utilization \((\%)\times\) areal S loading \((\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\)
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathrm{Areal~capacity(mAh\cdot cm^{-2})=}}\\&{\mathrm{(C_{T}\times S~utilization\times S~loading/1000)}}\end{array}
\]
where TSC represents the theoretical specific capacity of sulfur, which is \(1675\ \mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\). Areal sulfur loading and sulfur utilization are measured in unit of \(\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and percentage \((\%)\), respectively. For example, if sulfur utilization and areal sulfur loading are \(80\%\) and \(5\ \mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), separately, the resulting areal capacity will be approximately \(6.7\ \mathrm{mAh\cdot cm^{-2}}\). In order to reach an areal capacity of more than \(4\ \mathrm{mAh\cdot cm^{-2}}\), areal sulfur loading needs to be improved to \(5\ \mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at a minimum with a sulfur utilization of about \(50\%\). Typically, increasing areal sulfur loading within the cathode involves an increase in electrode thickness, which in turn raises the resistance to incoming lithium ion and limits the movement of electron. Thus, while a high reversible capacity could be obtained with low areal sulfur loading, this does not necessarily mean that similar electrochemical performance could be attained with higher areal sulfur loading in practical cell [62, 79]. The gravimetric energy densities of electrodes with low areal sulfur loading are very lower than those of commercial LIBs, but such experiments are still meaningful for studying the reaction mechanism and conducting analytical research on LSBs. To obtain high sulfur loading in the cathode, it is necessary to explore new designs of
electrodes and new conductive electrode materials that can accommodate the active species, namely sulfur, without having a significant impact on the conductivity of the electrodes.
2.2.5 Electrolyte-to-sulfur (E/S) ratio and electrolyte-to-capacity (E/C) ratio
The E/S ratio and E/C ratio are two significant parameters in LSBs that have not obtained sufficient attentions. The electrolyte is an inert component that makes up the maximum weight fraction of LSBs, which is crucial for determining the final energy density and is the most significant factor that can be altered to improve specific energy. The amount of electrolyte also affects sulfur utilization, cycling stability, and CE since electrolyte is directly associated with soluble polysulfide [62]. Thus, the relationship between the volume of electrolyte applied in a battery and the amount of sulfur in the cathode should be considered to maximize specific energy. The E/S ratio \((\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1})\) accurately reflects this dependence and determines both the energy density and electrochemical performance of LSBs. To achieve excellent electrochemical performance and energy density, further efforts should be made to control the E/S ratio below \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\) [80].
The traditional ether-based electrolyte could dissolve up to \(8\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) of sulfur to form PSs in solutions [70]. Under low E/S ratio condition, the concentration of dissolved PSs could be driven to the electrolyte saturation point. This onset of electrolyte saturation caused by PSs impedes the further dissolution of active materials, restraining the transformation of residual sulfur into \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). Therefore, under finite electrolytic condition, sulfur utilization and capacity declined in a battery can be reduced. The interactive relationship between the amount of electrolyte and sulfur utilization is expressed as the E/C ratio, which is defined as the amount of electrolyte used per unit discharging capacity delivered by the battery. Although the E/C ratio cannot be directly controlled, it is an effective indicator for determining specific energy, as it associates the inert weight of the electrolyte with the capacity of a battery. Higher sulfur utilization can maximize specific energy, reflected by the minimum E/C ratio. For instance, the cathode working at the E/S ratio as low as \(2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\) could achieve the energy density of \(325~\mathrm{\:Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\) with the sulfur utilization of only \(60\%\) that corresponds to the E/C ratio of \(2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mAh}^{-1}\). When the sulfur utilization rate reaches \(90\%\) , corresponding to an E/C ratio of \(3.3~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mAh}^{-1}\), the same energy density can still be achieved by increasing the E/S ratio to \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\). E/C ratio is a measure of the electrolyte effectiveness for effectively utilizing the given cathode architecture, which is emphasized as indicated the above-mentioned example [80]. As described above, adjusting some characteristics such as wettability and interaction, can minimize the E/C ratio. Therefore, besides controlling the E/S ratio below \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\), designing the cathode to maintain an E/C ratio below \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mAh}^{-1}\) is crucial for realistically and effectively evaluating the improvement effect.
## 2.2.6 Negative-to-positive (N/P) capacity ratio
The ratio between the areal capacity of lithium metal anode (negative electrode) and that of the sulfur cathode (positive electrode) is described as the N/P ratio. Ideally, the Li-S full battery should operate at an N/P ratio of 1.8 [81]. Nevertheless, excessive lithium is needed to compensate for the loss of lithium because of the electrolyte's decomposition and the formation of a SEI. Moreover, utilizing excess lithium penalizes the specific energy. When N/P ratio is close to 1, specific energy improves from \(100\%\) to \(200\%\), relying on the E/S ratio applied. To enhance these improvements brought about through lowering the E/S ratio and the E/C ratio, further efforts should be made to apply thin lithium foils with an N/P ratio less than 5 [80]. This would prominently
enhance both the gravimetric energy density and the volumetric energy density and provide a realistic view of cycling life, as typical Li-S batteries apply thick lithium foils with an N/P ratio greater than or equal to 20 as anode.
## 2.2.7 Voltage window
LSBs are typically operated with a voltage range of \(1.5{-}3.0\ \mathrm{V}\) for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i_{2}}\) in order to take advantage of the capacity produced through the chemical reactions between elemental sulfur and its final product, lithium sulfide. The voltage window, particularly the discharge cut-off voltage, acts as a vital role in the formation of solid PSs, including \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\), within Li-S batteries, and can significantly affect both their cycling stability and energy density [13]. Presently, various research studies have been conducted to explore the use of different voltage windows for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i_{2}}\). For instance, the discharge cut-off voltage may be shifted from 1.5 to \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\) to prevent irreversible capacity loss from the \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) additive [82]. The \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) additive is typically employed in ether-based electrolyte to protect the Li metal anode, inhibiting polysulfide shuttling, and reaching high discharging/charging efficiency. However, at discharging cut-off voltages below \(1.7\ \mathrm{~V~}\), \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) undergoes irreversible reduction on the surface of cathode, which can impede the subsequent redox process's reaction kinetics and lead to poor cycling stability. Therefore, in electrolytes containing \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) additive, a discharge cut-off voltage of \(1.8~\mathrm{V}\) is optimal to maintain excellent reversibility of LSBs [62]. Similarly, when determining the discharge cut-off voltage, it is imperative to take into account the reduction potential of the electrolyte solvent. For instance, the reduction potential of the typical carbonate solvent stands at approximately \(0.7\,\mathrm{~V~}\) [83]. Moreover, for the anode's current collector, a lower cut-off voltage may prompt the formation of a Li alloy during the reduction process. That is, at a lower potential \((<0.5\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. Li), the creation of a Li-Al alloy on the Al foil might occur, leading to the development of cracks in the SEI [84, 85]. In addition, when oxides, sulfides, and hydroxides are selected as sulfur encapsulation materials, only when their charge cut-off voltage is between 2.4 and \(3.05\mathrm{~V~}\), can they enhance the redox reaction between active sulfur species while inhibiting LiPSs from shuttling. All in all, the optimal voltage range is usually a compromise between the cycle life and energy density of the LSB [86].
## 2.2.8 Electrode–electrolyte interface (EEI)
To date, severe shuttle effect and dendrite growth remain as the bottleneck in manufacturing of LSBs. Regrettably, the interface issue between the electrodes and electrolytes is more prominent in LSBs compared to conventional Li-rich layered transition metal oxide cathodes. Consequently, there remains an urgent need for advanced interface engineering to facilitate high-performance LSBs [87, 88]. First, the EEI should possess mechanical, chemical, and electrochemical stability, coupled with excellent ion conductivity. A consistent and durable ion transport network is vital to facilitate fast and efficient charging and discharging processes in batteries, significantly impacting the extension of the lifespan of LSBs [89]. Specifically, the interface situated on the anode should feature an electrically insulated inert interface to prevent further reactions between the electrolyte and the directly contacted lithium metal anode [90]. To address this issue, a wave of efforts have been dedicated to implementing an effective interface strategy. For instance, adjusting the active material size to increase the contact area and constructing gradient structures to reduce interface impedance have been proved [91]. Besides, some certain fluorine additives and gradient structures can ensure stable ion pathways while establishing high-performance EEI layer. The ideal scenario would involve additives possessing environmentally
friendly traits, minimal processing costs, and abundant raw materials [92, 93]. Notably, MOF-derived transition metal oxide/carbon composite materials can effectively mitigate the polysulfide shuttle. This is attributed to their porous structure, which not only enhances the adsorption capacity for polysulfides but also addresses the issue of uneven ion transfer in lithium metal anodes [94]. In conclusion, the pursuit of a robust and reliable EEI is critical for achieving dendrite-free lithium deposition and curtailing the shuttle effect in high-energy density LMBs.
## 2.2.9 Cycle life
The widespread implementation of Li-S batteries demands their excellent long-term stability, with cycle life being a vital indicator for evaluating their performance. Poor reversibility, often leading to short cycle life, is the key technical challenge that needs to be addressed. Cycle life is generally linked to electrode stability during repeated cycles [71]. Volumetric expansion during cycling typically leads to the decrease of electrode stability and further causes rapid capacity decay due to active material's electrical insulation. Additionally, an uncontrolled reaction in the interface between the electrode and electrolyte has long been considered as a major cause of capacity fading in lithium batteries [69]. Notably, the migration of dissolved and reduced PSs from the lithium metal anode can result in continuous and irreversible active material loss, particularly during initial cycling, leading to a rapid capacity decrease [95]. Thus, it is crucial to devise strategies to encapsulate PSs effectively into the cathodes for achieving long-term Li-S battery stability. Besides electrode stability, cycle life is also affected by S content and loading, E/S ratio, and cycle conditions, such as the voltage window.
## 2.2.10 Safety
Undoubtedly, safety cannot be ignored or underestimated, and it is one of the most essential requirements for lithium batteries. Certification must be obtained before they can be used [96]. In addition to the highly oxidized and reduced materials present in lithium batteries, poor heat dissipation is an intrinsic drawback of organic electrolytes. Overcharge and external short-circuit can stimulate spontaneous reactions that generate heat, leading to fire or explosion [69, 97]. Therefore, it is necessary to ensure safe operation of lithium batteries. The application of lithium metal as the anode material in LSBs is essential because of its high theoretical specific capacity [98, 99]. However, the formation of lithium dendrites during repeated cycling in lithium batteries is a concern. The growing of Li dendrites is uncontrollable, and they can penetrate the separator, causing short-circuiting, which is a major safety issue and the biggest obstacle to practical application. In recent years, there has been a necessity to develop lithium metal anodes to meet the ever-growing demand for high energy, and diverse methods have been studied to resolve security issues and make Li metal anodes a feasible technology [100].
## 2.2.11 Economic cost
Apart from the aforementioned evaluation metrics, economic cost deserves more attention and plays a vital role in determining whether a technology is suitable for practical application. For the commercialization of LSBs, the low-cost and natural abundance of elemental sulfur are among their main advantages, which cannot be compensated for by complex synthesis methods that significantly increase the cost of sulfur cathodes [62]. For example, Berg et al. compared cost and performance of different LIBs, LSBs, and lithium-air batteries (LABs) based on an energy–cost model [101]. Regarding LIBs cost, the authors compared layered lithium-rich nickel manganese cobalt oxide (LR-NMC)C], lithium nickel manganese oxide (LNMO)C], lithium nickel cobalt aluminum oxide (NCA)C, \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\) (LCO)C, lithium iron phosphate (LFP)C, and lithium cobalt phosphate[C cell chemistries and derived estimates that range from 191 to 295 US\\(kWh-1. Concerning the active material price, the calculated averages for materials such as LNMO and LFP are the lowest, while the high cobalt materials \)\mathrm{LiCoPO_{4}}\( (LCP) and LCO display the highest price, despite their provision of high cycle life and low self-discharge. In contrast, the LSBs and LABs exhibit costs of only 154 and 105 US\\)kWh-1, respectively. Hence, the potential replacement of LIBs in vehicles with lower-cost LSBs hinges on overcoming challenges such as the poor cycle life of the latter, specifically in achieving a durability target of 1000 cycles or sustaining well performance over 15 years [102]. Therefore, the cost of batteries has a certain impact on the materials' price and the entire cyclic life [103]. Moreover, apart from decreasing the E/S ratio and lithium excess, increasing the cyclical life of soft-packing LSBs can also reduce the total device's cost.
oxide \((\mathrm{NCA})\mathrm{C},\) , \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{LCO})|\mathrm{C},\) lithium iron phosphate (LFP)|C, and lithium cobalt phosphate|C cell chemistries and derived estimates that range from 191 to 295 \(\mathrm{US}\mathbb{S}\mathrm{{\cdot}k W h^{-1}}\) . Concerning the active material price, the calculated averages for materials such as LNMO and LFP are the lowest, while the high cobalt materials \(\mathrm{LiCoPO_{4}}\) (LCP) and LCO display the highest price, despite their provision of high cycle life and low selfdischarge. In contrast, the LSBs and LABs exhibit costs of only 154 and \(105\;\mathrm{US}\mathbb{S}\mathrm{{\cdot}k W h^{-1}}\) , respectively. Hence, the potential replacement of LIBs in vehicles with lower-cost LSBs hinges on overcoming challenges such as the poor cycle life of the latter, specifically in achieving a durability target of 1000 cycles or sustaining well performance over 15 years [102]. Therefore, the cost of batteries has a certain impact on the materials' price and the entire cyclic life [103]. Moreover, apart from decreasing the E/S ratio and lithium excess, increasing the cyclical life of soft-packing LSBs can also reduce the total device's cost.
Based on the above discussion, several parameters influence the energy density of LSBs, and some of them are not given enough attention. More reliable and safe LSBs should have high energy density and power density, long cyclic life, high safety, and low economic cost, which are all related to the various battery components. Thus, the following criteria must be satisfied to evaluate the parameters for high energy density: (1) sulfur content higher than 70% and sulfur loading higher than 5 mg·cm-2 on the cathode; (2) E/S ratio below 5 μL·mg-1, E/C ratio below 5 μL·mAh-1, and N/P ratio below 5; (3) discharging cut-off voltage higher than 1.7 V for the LiNO3-containing electrolyte; (4) easy-to-use electrodes made from low-cost materials and simple processes. In summary, considering these parameters comprehensively is crucial to boost the research of LSBs from laboratory scale to commercialization.
## 3 The application of MOFs-related cathode materials for Li-S batteries
Because of the porous microstructure, ultra-large accessible specific surface area, and adjustable functional group, MOFs-based materials have received the attentions of investigators as potential cathode hosts for LSBs and have made significant progress. Particularly, MOFs-related materials could effectively restrain the dissolution and diffusion of polysulfide in the electrolytes. In this section, four categories of MOFs-related materials applied for LSBs, i.e., pristine MOFs, MOFs composites, MOFs derivatives, and MOFs derivative composites (Table 1), are focused. The electrochemical performances of these materials as cathode hosts of LSBs are summarized and discussed in details.
## 3.1 Pristine MOFs
Pristine MOFs with the regular array of inorganic metal and organic components with the crystallographic structure have shown great potential in storing active sulfur with chain type structures prior to the discharge, while effectively trapping PSs through the chemical affinity and accommodating volumetric change upon the discharging [5, 137, 138]. The pioneering work of MOFs applied in LSBs as a sulfur host can date back to 2011 when Tarascon et al. reported the use of a mesoporous Cr-related MOF named MIL-100 (Cr) (MIL, Matérial Institut Lavoisier) as the host material for sulfur impregnation. Although successful impregnation of 48 wt.% sulfur was achieved within the pore of MIL-100 (Cr) with the large specific surface areas of 1485 m²·g⁻¹, the battery exhibited poor cycling stability because of the weak bond between the polysulfide and oxygenated MOF group [26].
Subsequent research has mainly focused on elucidating the influence of the pore structure and particle size of MOFs within
the electrochemical process of LSBs. For instance, Hong and colleagues [104] fabricated a series of cage-like Cu-MOFs (CuTDPAT) with a size range from \(\sim\,100\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(\sim\,1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) through microwave synthesis. Owing to the confined nano-space effect of the cage structure, and abundant \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) open metal sites or/and the N functional sites from the organic ligand, the optimized CuTDPAT with the size of \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) as cathode material for LSBs delivered a reversible capacity of about \(745\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) over 500 cycles. Liu et al. [105] synthesized a Mn-MOF with the structure of cube cages with a diameter of \(14.29\ \mathrm{~\AA~}\) by a solvothermal method. Such a \(\mathrm{S@Mn-C}C s\) electrode displayed an initial reversible capacity of \(1460\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2~\mathrm{C}\) and excellent rate capability for LSBs (Table 1). These results should be ascribed to the synergic effect from the physical capture and chemical adsorption of PSs by the open metal site of the MOFs. Thus, the cage-like mesopores, crosslinked pores, and open metal sites in the structure of the above-mentioned MOFs favour the confinement of PSs and ion diffusion. Furthermore, a small particle size could increase the surface absorption sites, improving the ability to lead polysulfide into the pores.
Consistent with above discussions, Geng et al. [51] proposed a bimetallic Al/Cu-MOF by a one-step hydrothermal process to achieve a high-adsorption capacity towards LiPSs (Fig. 3(a)). Due to the stronger polysulfide adsorption, it exhibited better electrochemical performance compared to monometallic Al-MOF for LSBs. Figure 3(b) illustrates the interaction mechanism between LiPSs and host materials including Al-MOF and Al/Cu-MOF. As a result, for Al/Cu-MOF, the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x}\) molecule can be easily seized by \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) due to the strong binding energy with \(\mathrm{S}_{n}{}^{2-}\) and lower steric hindrance, compared with that of six coordinate \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) ions. In another study, Yang et al. [52] introduced one-dimensional (1D) \(\pi\) -d conjugated Ni-MOF-1D as the effective S cathode for LSBs. Combined with experiment and density functional theory (DFT) calculations (see Figs. 3(c)–3(e)), it was demonstrated that Ni-MOF-1D not only possessed high electrical conductivity to overcome the insulation property of S, \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S},\) and most polar sulfur-related electrode and maximize the sulfur utilization, but also served as an effective bifunctional electrocatalyst to promote the formation and decomposition of
much higher than that of other MOFs, and the small volume expansion of about \(34\%\) sulfur. From the aspect of band structure and density of state, V-MOF also displayed metal property or a small band gap. It is worth noting that the exfoliating energy of V-MOF from the multi-layer structure was smaller than that of graphene. Thus, 2D V-MOF was expected to become a potential cathode material for Li-S batteries with good performance and stability.
Despite the utilization of some unaltered MOFs that possess favorable conductivity and potent adsorption attributes towards PSs in LSBs, the majority of pristine MOFs exhibit innate electrical insulation with a conductivity of less than \(10^{-10}\;\mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and limited stability. Consequently, their electrochemical activity is hindered by low-rate properties and low sulfur utilization, as indicated by
previous studies [140–142]. As a result, it is imperative to consider the development and design of pristine MOFs with superior electrical conductivity and stability in future research.
## 3.2 MOF composites
To enhance the practical application of MOFs in sulfur cathode material for high-performance Li-S batteries, the incorporation of pristine MOFs with highly conductive and mechanically robust carbonaceous materials, including graphene and CNTs, and conductive polymers such as polypyrrole (PPy) and poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) is regarded as an efficient and straightforward approach. This method not only elevates the conductivity of the electrode but also reinforces the structural stability of the sulfur cathode.
## 3.2.1 Composite with graphene
3.2.1 Composite with grapheneGraphene, discovered by Novoselov and Geim, possesses high specific surface areas, superior conductivity and charge-carrier mobility, good flexibility, and excellent mechanical strength property [143, 144]. Although the chemical and physical property of graphene is similar with that of CNTs, it exhibits decent electrical conductivity because of the specific 2D structure. Hence, the combination of graphene with pristine MOFs is a potential technique to significantly improve the electrochemical performances of LSBs as the sulfur cathode. The unique structure of composite material with a high surface area and a conductive shell can guarantee good dispersion of S in the composite and minimise the loss of polysulfide in the electrolytes [34]. For instance, Zhao and colleagues [20] utilized graphene sheets to encapsulate the surface of the MIL-101(Cr)/S composite (GNSMIL-101(Cr)/S) through strong electrostatic interaction for building the conductive bridge to transfer electrons and the physical barrier to inhibit the dissolution of polysulfide in liquid electrolytes. As a result, they achieved prominently enhanced electrochemical performances, such as cyclability and rate capability (Table 1). Moreover, Baumann et al. [145] combined the advantage of a Zr-related MOF-808 loading with sulfur with graphene/ethyl cellulose to obtain the MOF-808@S/GEC composite based electrode, which delivered even higher volumetric capacity than that of the conventional Super-P/PVDF composites electrode. In addition, reduced graphene oxide (rGO) is one major derivative of graphene due to its exceptional conductivity and mechanical strength. In recent years, researchers have combined rGO with various MOFs, which possess specific surface areas and adjustable porosity, to construct MOF-rGO composite materials. These materials have shown great promising as sulfur cathodes in LSBs to meet the growing energy demands of modern society. For instance, Wu et al. [110] successfully synthesized a three-dimensional (3D) hierarchical structure MOF (e.g., MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-53(Al))-on-rGO compartment through the in-situ combined and reduced method (see Fig. 3(h)). The MOF-on-rGO compartment addressed the issue of weak electrical conductivity from MOFs, while MOFs provided enough channels for mass transfer. Overall, graphene and its derivatives (G/GD) possess decent mechanical property and high electrical conductivity, making the MOF-G/GD composite a promising cathode material for LSBs. This research also opens new avenues for exploring the application of MOF-G/GD composites in other types of batteries.
## 3.2.2 Composite with CNTs
3.2.2 Composite with CNTsCNTs are considered as curled graphene, which also exhibit good electrical properties. Moreover, CNTs possess excellent porosity, thermal conductivity, and stability, as well as mechanical properties, which have made them receive significant attention as one of the most potential flexible electrode materials in the electronics and electrochemistry fields [146, 147]. In recent years, the combination of CNTs with different MOFs to fabricate hybrid electrodes has been widely employed for LSBs. Zhang et al. [111] investigated the performances of 3D porous Zn MOF (ZIF-8)@CNT network via the solvothermal procedure as a cathode material for LSBs, and two different hybrid structures named S-ZIF-8@CNTs \((\sim\,40\,\mathrm{\nm})\) and L-ZIF-8@CNTs ZIF-8 \((\sim\,500\,\mathrm{\nm})\) through controlling the concentration of ZIF-8 precursor can be obtained (Figs. 3(j) and 3(k)). As a result, the resultant \(\mathrm{S@L-ZIF-8@CNTs}\) cathode delivered the outstanding areal capacity of \(11\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) as well as remarkable cyclic stability with the capacity retention of \(\sim90\%\) over 500 cycles, which was even better than other MOF-related hybrid electrodes.
Furthermore, Zhang et al. [41] also explored the performance of DCNTs@ZIF-8 (small size) hybrid through the in-situ growing and room-temperature dry process as the sulfur cathode material in the LSBs, which delivered the high areal capacity of \(10.7\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and volumetric capacity of \(676\,\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\).
Prussian blue (PB) analogues, as a special MOF, were used by Shen and co-workers [112] to combine with CNT for constructing a novel PB/CNT hybrid nanocomposite with the 3D "necklace"-like microstructures as the sulfur cathode material for high-performance LSBs. In this architecture, 3D CNT continuous conductive skeleton acted as the efficient ion/electron diffusion path, and the monodisperse PB nano-cube could provide plentiful Lewis's acid sites, efficaciously entrapping LiPSs. Thus, the as-prepared S@PB/CNT electrode demonstrated good electrochemical performance with stable cyclic stability, high reversibility, and rapid kinetic. Besides, Rana et al. [45] selected the antiferroelectric perovskite dimethylammonium zinc formate (DMAZF)-MOF with Zn-metal site and tuned porosity to enhance its performance when in conjunction with conductive CNTs. These authors prepared a cathode using DMAZF/CNTs/sulfur and investigated its potential in improving the performance of LSBs (Fig. 3(i)). The obtained outstanding electrochemical performances characteristics could be ascribed to the nanoporous structure of the DMAZF with Zn-site and conductive CNTs. Thus, CNTs have been widely utilized to enhance rate performance and increase the sulfur loading of MOF-related host materials, benefiting from the good electrical conductivity and high specific surface area.
## 3.2.3 Composite with conductive polymers
3.2.3 Composite with conductive polymersConductive polymer combined with MOFs is another effective approach to assemble complex structure for improving the performance of MOF-related cathode materials [148]. On the one hand, conductive polymer significantly enhanced the weak conductivity of S based cathode, accelerated the electronic transmissions, and further improved the electrochemical performances. On the other hand, conductive polymer has polar bond containing \(\mathrm{=C-H}\), N-H, and so on, which could chemically anchor PSs and restrain the shuttling effect [50, 149]. Among these conductive polymers, due to the strong charge storage capability and high conductivity \((10{-}100\;\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\), PPy has received great attentions in the electrochemical energy storage field. In addition, PPy possesses the ability to maintain the initial states of the polymers because of the reversible electrochemical redox reaction upon the repeated charging/discharging process [150]. Geng et al. [39] utilized a flexible aqueous phase dissolution process following a facile melt-diffusion method to obtain ZIF-67 composite impregnated with S covered by a PPy layer (ZIF-67-S-PPy) with a favorable hollow structure. To further improve the stability and catalytic activity, Chen et al. [114] synthesized two types of Ce-MOFs, including Ce-MOF-808 and Ce-UiO-66-BPDC, with dissimilar open metal sites and then covered by the PPy layer as a cathode host. The Ce-MOF-808 with a unique micro-mesoporous architecture and a large specific surface area contributing to inhibit shuttling effect and buffer the volumetric change via the physical adsorption and the steady channel structure, special adsorption and catalytical effect of cerium sites, and the coated PPy layer adsorbing PSs and enhancing the intrinsic inferior conductivity. In addition, PPy nanotubes (PPyNTs) are also attracting the growing attention owing to their good properties with good electrical conductivity, low-density, and high aspect ratio. Li et al. [40] designed PPyNTs coated by nanoscale arrays of UIO-66 (PPyNTs@UIO-66). The excellent electrochemical performance should be attributed to the synergistic effect between UIO-66 with porosity and a high surface area and PPyNTs with decent electrical conductivity.
## 3.2.4 Composite with other materials
3.2.4 Composite with other materialsWith the exception of the aforementioned conductive materials, CC is also utilized to enhance the conductivity of MOF-related sulfur cathode. Yang et al. [38] created and produced a novel 3D heterogeneous sulfur host, a ternary hybrid material composed of ZIF-8 (Ni MOF) deposited onto carbon cloth, labeled as Ni-ZIF-8@CC, to increase the electrochemical performance of LSBs. The as-obtained Ni-ZIF-8@CC/S electrode still maintained an areal capacity of \(5.3\,\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) even at the S loading of \(5.5\,\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which was superior to the reported MOFs-related sulfur electrodes. The excellent performance can be ascribed to the multicomponent heterostructure, including the pristine ZIF-8 with cage-like pore which efficaciously traps PSs, nickel species with catalytic ability, which not only generate strong adsorption towards polysulfide but also facilitate the redox kinetic of PSs, and carbon cloth with an open structure that improves electron transportation and adapts to high sulfur mass loading. In conclusion, conductive materials including graphene, CNTs, and polymers have been incorporated into MOFs to create MOF-composites that can compensate for the deficiencies in the insulation properties of MOFs. In addition to using MOF/sulfur composites with conductive additives, it is also feasible to design and synthesize innovative conductive MOFs or functionalized MOFs that can serve as the materials for S cathodes in LSBs.
## 3.3 MOF derivatives
3.3 MOF derivativesPristine MOFs, either as a self-sacrificed template or a precursor, could be directly converted into C-based materials, metal compounds, and their composites through a high-temperature calcination process. These MOF derivatives possess a hierarchically porous structure, improved conductivity, high porosity, and rich polar/catalytic sites, which can enhance the performance of LSBs [20, 151–153]. Specifically, the uniform porous carbon layer with remarkable electrical conductivity facilitates the transport of ions/electrons and physically confines PSs. Moreover, the incorporated metal compounds' abundant polar/catalytic sites could not only chemically immobilize polysulfide but also efficiently catalyze these conversion reactions of sulfur species. Additionally, high porosity can improve the sulfur loading capacity [154–156]. In the following section, a detailed review of various MOF derivatives as cathode materials in LSBs will be provided.
## 3.3.1 MOF-derived carbon materials
3.3.1 MOF-derived carbon materialsCarbon materials, derived from MOFs, are typically obtained through high-temperature carbonization of the corresponding MOFs precursor under an inert atmosphere, which is commonly used to produce porous carbon materials [10, 157]. Compared with pristine MOFs, MOF-derived carbon materials used as sulfur hosts exhibit some advantages, such as higher structural strength and lower solubility. For instance, Li et al. [116] designed a new 3D porous Co and N-doping graphitic carbon (Co-N-GC) composite using ZIF-67 as the precursor. The outstanding cell performance (Table 1) should be ascribed to abundant uniform micropores and cobalt nanoparticle (NP) homogeneously distributed in nitrogen-doping C frameworks, which were highly effective in physically and electro-catalytically trapping PSs and promoting electrons and ions transfer upon the discharging/charging process. Besides, Zhou et al. [117] utilized bimetal core–shell MOF (ZIF-8 core and ZIF-67 shell) as the precursor to compose CNTs transplanted N-doping carbon@GC nanocage (CNT-NC@GC) through a pyrolysis process based on the different thermal stabilities of ZIF-67 and ZIF-8. These matrix exhibited multiple structures advantages when employed as the S cathode of LSBs: (1) The porous carbon shell with a large specific surface area and high porosity provides ample void for high S loading and simultaneously reduces the ions/electrons transport path, leading to the increased utilization of active materials. (2) Insitu-drew CNTs into the internal/outside surface of the nanocage form the 3D integrated conductive networks throughout the whole electrode, buffering the volume expansion, enhancing electronic conductivity and electrolyte penetration, promoting the full use of sulfur, and improving electron/ion transfer. (3) The nitrogen-doped and embedded metallic cobalt nanoparticle decorated in the tip of CNTs or in the GC shell surface facilitates the immobilization of soluble PSs. (4) The modified Co nanoparticle possesses the ability to accelerate the transition of LiPSs through electrocatalysis effect and chemical adsorption, beneficially promoting the redox kinetic reaction of PSs and improving cyclic stability. Also, Capkova et al. [118] investigated the performance of activated MOF-76(Gd)(AC) and carbonized MOF-76(Gd)(C) as sulfur cathodes for LSBs. The better performance of MOF-76(Gd)(C) can be attributed to the MOF-76(Gd)(C) sulfur host's stronger interactions with sulfur and polysulfide particles, effectively trapping the PSs, inhibiting the shuttling effect, and enhancing the cycling stability during the cycling process.
cathode of LSBs: (1) The porous carbon shell with a large specific surface area and high porosity provides ample void for high S loading and simultaneously reduces the ions/electrons transport path, leading to the increased utilization of active materials. (2) Insitu-drew CNTs into the internal/outside surface of the nanocage form the 3D integrated conductive networks throughout the whole electrode, buffering the volume expansion, enhancing electronic conductivity and electrolyte penetration, promoting the full use of sulfur, and improving electron/ion transfer. (3) The nitrogen-doped and embedded metallic cobalt nanoparticle decorated in the tip of CNTs or in the GC shell surface facilitates the immobilization of soluble PSs. (4) The modified Co nanoparticle possesses the ability to accelerate the transition of LiPSs through electrocatalysis effect and chemical adsorption, beneficially promoting the redox kinetic reaction of PSs and improving cyclic stability. Also, Capkova et al. [118] investigated the performance of activated MOF-76(Gd)(AC) and carbonized MOF-76(Gd)(C) as sulfur cathodes for LSBs. The better performance of MOF-76(Gd)(C) can be attributed to the MOF\(76(\mathrm{Gd})(\mathrm{C})\) sulfur host's stronger interactions with sulfur and polysulfide particles, effectively trapping the PSs, inhibiting the shuttling effect, and enhancing the cycling stability during the cycling process.
A single atom catalyst (SAC) refers to a monodispersed atom that is anchored onto a solid substrate. SACs possess certain properties, including a theoretical \(100\%\) atom utilization efficiency and a combination of inhomogeneous and homogeneous catalysis [158]. Due to the high exposed atom efficiency, they are regarded as an excellent choice for the super extensive applications. For example, benefiting from the synergic effect from the uniformly dispersed Fe single atom (FeSA) and N-abundant porous carbon using the ZIF-8 precursor, the FeSA-CN/S electrode could obviously promote polysulfide conversion and promote the chemical adsorption of LiPSs, as designed by Wang et al. [119]. Moreover, Li et al. [43] synthesized N-doping carbon dodecahedron supported Co single atom (Co-SAs@NC) related to a binary Co-Zn MOF precursor for the sulfur host of LSBs (see Fig. 4(a)). They revealed that the special \(\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}\) moiety in the Co-SAs@NC host material could provide electrocatalytically active sites to capture soluble LiPSs and electrochemically catalyse reversible LiPSs conversions, which would effectively alleviate the polysulfide shuttling effect.
Due to the good conductivity, high specific surface areas, high porosity, and uniform heteroatom doping, carbonaceous material derived from MOFs has been identified as potential sulfur hosts for LSBs. These carbon materials possess favourable characteristics that facilitate electrons and ions transfers and mitigate the volume expansion of the sulfur cathode upon charging/discharging cycles. Additionally, their controllable chemical active sites can adsorb PSs, thus improving the kinetic of the conversion reaction. However, the carbonization process of MOFs poses some challenges. On the one hand, the inherent structure of MOFs may be compromised, and on the other hand, controlling the structures and pore sizes of MOF-derived carbonaceous materials could be challenging. Moreover, the pyrolyzation of organic ligand may render MOF-derived carbonaceous materials unable to adsorb PSs. Therefore, it is important to explore the suitable condition including calcination temperature for well-controlled carbonization of MOFs.
## 3.3.2 MOFs-derived metal compounds
3.3.2 MOFs-derived metal compoundsThe utilization of MOFs as polar hosts for LSBs extends beyond the scope of carbon materials derived from MOFs. MOF-derived metal compounds exhibit stronger chemical adsorption capabilities towards PSs when compared to their MOF-derived
carbon material counterparts [10]. Furthermore, MOF-derived metal compounds with distinct morphology and highly porous structure can be tailored by precisely controlling the annealing atmosphere and condition, yielding metal oxide, hydroxides, sulfide, nitride, phosphide, carbides, and binary composites [159].
Metal oxide possesses a highly polar surface and typically exhibits strong chemical interactions with LiPSs due to the presence of the oxygen anion \(\left(\mathrm{O}^{2-}\right)\). In general, the metal oxide derived from MOFs was fabricated through cooling or annealing at air atmosphere or calcination at inert atmosphere using MOFs as precursor [160–163]. Among all the possible metal oxide candidates, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) has garnered significant attention due to its abundance in nature, low cost, and non-toxicity. Moreover, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) can effectively chemically absorb sulfur and polysulfide species. In a study by Li et al. [120], a nano-porous \(\mathrm{Co-TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}\) composite
with the tablet-like structure derived from the Ti-MOF (MIL-125) through the simple impregnation of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) and carbonization process. The conductive carbon network can efficiently trap PSs via physical adsorption. The polar \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles are capable of chemically immobilizing sulfur species and PSs, while the catalytic metal Co nanoparticles could promote the conversions of adsorbed long-chain LiPSs into short-chain sulfide, thereby improving the sulfur utilization and the reaction kinetics. Thus, the resulting \(\mathrm{Co-TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}/\) sulfur cathode demonstrated excellent electrochemical performances, with a decent initial discharging capacity of \(1245\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Table 1), which was higher than that of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{S}\) cathode. Furthermore, Yang et al. [121] designed hollow micro-cuboid \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) with a hierarchal lasagna-like structure using MIL-47 (V MOF) as the precursor via hydrothermal synthesis followed by the calcination process.
Importantly, even at an ultrahigh sulfur loading of \(7.9~\mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\), the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{S}\) composite cathode could still maintain a delightful areal capacity of \(3.66~\mathrm{mAh\cdot cm^{-2}}\) over 500 cycles at \(1~\mathrm{C}\) (Table 1). Such outstanding electrochemical performances would be ascribed to both \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) nanoparticles and the conductive 3D carbon framework in the structure, which could simultaneously suppress the shuttling effect within the charge/discharge cycling. Furthermore, the unique lasagna-like structure with suitable void would not only offer abundant site to increase the sulfur loading, but also greatly alleviate the volumetric change upon the cycling.
Metal hydroxides, as a type of polar material, possess rich hydroxyl and hydrophilic groups on the surface, which make them capable of providing efficient chemical entrapment for LiPSs. Additionally, metal hydroxides exhibit electrocatalytic properties, which contribute to improving the kinetic of the redox reaction of LiPSs, and have high anion exchange capability that can entrap PSs and effectively suppress their dissolution into the organic electrolytes. For synthesis of metal hydroxides derived from MOFs, the MOFs were adopted as precursor, which were treated by hydrothermal or etching process [164–167]. In 2016, Zhang et al. [33] devised and fabricated double-shell nanocage comprised of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and layered double hydroxide \(\left(\mathrm{CH}@\mathrm{LDH}\right)\) utilizing ZIF-67 (Co MOF) as a sacrificial template (Fig. 4(b)). The resulting \(\mathrm{CH}@\mathrm{LDH}\) complex shell structure allowed for the maximum advantage of a hollow nanostructure to load a great quantity of sulfur and offered adequate self-functional surface to chemically bond with polysulfide, thereby suppressing the extrinsic dissolution. Two years later, they employed a similar synthesis method to prepare new hollow Ni/Fe layered double hydroxide polyhedron (Ni/Fe LDH) using the MIL-88A (Fe MOF) template (Fig. 4(d)) [123]. The \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Fe}\) LDH cathode exhibited a high discharging capacity of \(1091~\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) and a small capacity fading rate of \(0.04\%\) per cycle with 1000 cycles. This outcome could be ascribed to the synergistic effect from the hollow architecture and Ni/Fe LDH shells. Despite the advantages of these materials for the cathode of LSBs, the poor conductivity of metal hydroxide hinders the transporting of electrons/ions. Therefore, combining them with conductive materials such as carbon can improve overall conductivity. Additionally, the interactive mechanism between LiPSs and metal hydroxide is not yet fully understood and requires further investigation [165].
Metal sulfide typically consists of various metallic or half-metallic phases, which distinguish it from most metal oxides that are semi-conductor [168]. Therefore, metal sulfide exhibits higher electronic conductivity, stronger sulfiphilic property, and effective electro-catalysis for sulfur conversion reaction [161, 165]. Metal sulfide derived from MOFs was synthesized by sulphurization through hydrothermal or calcination process using MOFs as precursor S, thiourea, or thioacetamide as sulfur source [169–171]. \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\), with its stable and semi-metallic crystal phase, has a high conductivity of \(6.7\times10^{5}\) s·cm\(^{-1}\) [172]. Luo et al. [124] synthesized polar \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles embedded into nitrogen-doping porous carbon frameworks \(\left(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}\right)\) using polyhedron ZIF-67 (Co MOF) precursor. In this composite, nitrogen-doping porous carbon offers a conductive network that significantly reduces the resistance of electron/ion transferring within the cycle. Additionally, polar \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticle not only exhibits chemisorptive capability towards efficient LiPSs immobilization, but also plays a catalytical role in facilitating the oxidoreduction kinetic of soluble LiPSs into insoluble products. Consequently, the \(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}-\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}\) cathode demonstrated a degradation rate as low as \(0.064\%\) per cycle after 500 cycles at \(1~\mathrm{C}\). Importantly, this cell even exhibited a high initial capacity and superior cyclic performance with a high sulfur loading of \(5.25~\mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\). Furthermore, Seo et al. [125] successfully constructed \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles embedded in
the in-situ grown N-doping graphitic carbon nano-shell matrix \(\left(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{NGCNs}\right)\) with a "brain-coral-like" hollow architecture (see Fig. 4(d)). Accordingly, when assessed as an efficient sulfur host for LSBs, the \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{NGCNs}/\mathrm{S}\) with the sulfur content of \(75~\mathrm{wt.\%}\) exhibited an excellent initial discharging capacity of \(1300~\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\), which is much better than those of the \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{NGCNs}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}@\mathrm{NGCNs}\) electrodes (see Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)). Such an outstanding performance should be ascribed to chemically active \(\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\) nanoparticles, conductive N-doped carbon network with a mesoporous structure, and the dual hollow scaffolds. In addition, Seo's group [46] also reported polar \(\mathrm{VO}_{2}/\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) heterostructure uniformly embedded into a layered porous carbon \(\left(\mathrm{VO}_{2}/\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}@\mathrm{LPC}\right)\) derived from MIL-47 (V MOF) precursor through a one-step carbonization-sulfidation method (see Fig. 4(g)). Notably, \(\mathrm{VO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) exhibit high \((-2.13~\mathrm{eV})\) and moderate \((-1.41~\mathrm{eV})\) binding energies (vs. \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\)), respectively, and the metallic properties and 2D anisotropic morphology of \(\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) contribute significantly to its enhanced electron transfer capability. Additionally, because of the presence of \(\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) nanosheet and multiple porosity in the carbon layer structure, the pore volume and specific surface area of LPC would further raise, which made the as-obtained \(\mathrm{VO}_{2}/\mathrm{V}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}@\mathrm{LPC}\) own high sulfur mass loading (see Fig. 4(h)). As a result, this cathode with the sulfur content of \(70~\mathrm{wt.\%}\) delivered an excellent initial discharging capacity of \(1394.6~\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) and a superior cyclability with a reversible specific capacity of \(665~\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) after 1000 cycles at \(1~\mathrm{C}\).
Metal phosphides also hold promise as sulfur host materials because of the strong chemical interaction with polysulfide and fast catalytical conversion rates, which can help restrain the shuttle effect. The metal phosphides derived from MOFs were synthesized through phosphatizing process, in which ammonium dihydrogen phosphate was used as phosphorus source and MOFs was used as precursor [173]. Luo et al. [54] proposed a novel dual-template (ZIF-67@ZIF-8) strategy for in-situ preparation of abundant CoP and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}\right)\) NPs embedded onto the hollow nitrogen-doping carbon \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{NC}\right)\), as shown in Fig. 4(i). When used as a sulfur host, the resulting \(\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{NC}\) cathode with a sulfur content of \(82\%\) achieved impressive stability with a capacity decay of \(0.053\%\) per cycle at \(1~\mathrm{C}\) over 1250 cycles. The outstanding electrochemical performance could be ascribed to the synergetic effect of the rich electroactive multiphase interface and the exquisite hollow structure, which had a strong capture ability towards LiPSs, facilitating rapid ions/electrons transport as well as conversion kinetics of LiPSs (see Fig. 4(j)).
## 3.4 MOFs derivatives composites
Combining MOFs derivative with conductive and other material represents a promising approach for developing MOFs derivative composite with decreased mechanical stress and prevented self-aggregation, leading to excellent electrochemical performance. For instance, Chen et al. [37] synthesized N-doping porous carbon fastened onto graphene sheet (NPC/G), as shown in Fig. 5(a), and the resulted S-NPC/G cathode had good initial reversible capacity of \(1372~\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) and remarkable cycling stability due to the multifunctional carbon hybrid comprising of N-doping porous carbon nanoparticles with large surface areas and highly conductive graphene. Graphene oxide (GO), a graphene derivative, can also be combined with MOFs-derived carbon nanotube to enhance the performance of LSBs. Jiang et al. [126] proposed and fabricated \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) etching ZIF-67-derived CNT embedded in GO (Mg ZIF-67/CNT@GO) through the spray drying method. Such a composite with excellent electrical conductivity and hollow structure contributed to alleviating the volumetric fluctuation of sulfur. Furthermore, activated CC with excellent electrical conductivity, flexibility, and bendability can also
be combined with Co MOF derivatives to construct porous carbon cloth (PCC), as proposed by Fu et al. [127] (see Fig. 5(b)). Benefiting from the interconnected hierarchical porous structure, the as-prepared sulfur-impregnated PCC electrode exhibited better performance for LSBs than the sulfur-impregnated CC electrode.
To date, researchers have explored to use polydopamine (PDA) as a carbon source for improving the performance of LSBs when combined with MOF derivatives. PDA serves a dual purpose for protecting the MOFs derivative structure from collapse during the following process and offering sufficient cushion space for the volumetric expansion of sulfur. Additionally, the carbon framework from the carbonization of PDA enhances electrical conductivity and exhibits strong chemical absorption towards PSs. For instance, Sun et al. [128] prepared a multi yolk–shell hybrid structure consisting of Co nanoparticles embedded into N-doping carbon derived from ZIF-67 (Co MOF) as yolk and nitrogen
To date, researchers have explored to use polydopamine (PDA) as a carbon source for improving the performance of LSBs when combined with MOF derivatives. PDA serves a dual purpose for protecting the MOFs derivative structure from collapse during the following process and offering sufficient cushion space for the volumetric expansion of sulfur. Additionally, the carbon framework from the carbonization of PDA enhances electrical conductivity and exhibits strong chemical absorption towards PSs. For instance, Sun et al. [128] prepared a multi yolk–shell hybrid structure consisting of Co nanoparticles embedded into N-doping carbon derived from ZIF-67 (Co MOF) as yolk and nitrogen-doped hollow carbon generated from the carbonization of PDA as the shell (designated as Co-NC@N-HCSS) via the nano-confined growing method as the sulfur cathode for LSBs. The resulting cathode, with a sulfur content of 80.82 wt.%, delivered improved prolonged cyclic stability, which should be ascribed to the unique multi yolk–shell design. Beyond these, Xin et al. [131] fabricated highly distributed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) embedded with prismatic-like N-doping carbon tube \(\mathrm{(Fe_{3}O_{4}}\) -PNCT) related on the template of core–shell PW@PDA (prussian white: \(\mathrm{(Na_{3.1}Fe_{4}[I e(C N)_{6}]_{3}}\) , PW) through a calcination process (see Figs. 5(c) and 5(d)). \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -PNCT with the good conductivity and well-defined hollow structure could effectively promote electrons and ions transfers, alleviate the volumetric expansion and anchor PSs. More significantly, on the one hand, polar \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles as electrocatalyst efficiently adsorbed PSs in the conductive sulfur host surface. On the other hand, it also greatly accelerated the catalytical transformation of PSs. Consequently, the optimized \(\mathrm{S/Fe_{3}O_{4}}\) -PNCT-1 electrode
exhibited an excellent rate capacity of \(685~\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(5~\mathrm{C}\) and superior cyclic stability at \(5~\mathrm{C}\) (Table 1). Nevertheless, Zhang et al. [132] proposed and synthesized CoP nanoparticles embedded into N-doping carbon nanophage shell (CoP-HNC) with the porous architecture via a facile and efficient self-template strategy (see Fig. 5(g)), and the designed S/CoP-HNC cathode also achieved a superior rate capacity and enhanced cyclic stability over 1000 cycles at \(5~\mathrm{C}\) (see Figs. 5(e) and 5(f)). Figure 5(h) displays the optimized adsorbing configuration and the relevant energetic information of polysulfide specie on the surfaces of CoP (211) and Co (111) based on the DFT calculations, suggesting the facile LiPSs transformations for CoP. Such a delightful electrochemical performance could be attributed to the prominent structure advantages with high porosity and well-defined hollow structure and synergistic effect of the CoP nanoparticle and porous hollow nitrogen-doping carbon shells.
Beyond these, as displayed in Fig. 5(i), Li et al. [53] designed a hollow and free-standing \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) heterostructure encapsulated into the N-doping mesoporous C frameworks formed by PDA carbonization. The unique hollow architecture of the \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) heterojunction could provide the physical barrier to relieve the undue volumetric expansion of sulfur and offer adequate buffer space toward LiPSs. The N-doping C framework acted as a conductive framework and offered abundant space for fast electrons/ions diffusion. The polar \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{S}_{3}\) species showed strong chemical adsorption capacity towards LiPSs and served as intrinsically catalytical site to quicken the conversion of LiPSs. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) acted as nanocrystals to facilitate the reaction kinetics. Based on these superiorities, this cathode delivered an excellent prolonged cyclical stability with the capacity attenuation of \(0.04\%\) per cycle beyond 1000 cycles at \(2\,\mathrm{C}\).
In addition to PDA, melamine foam (MF) composed of melamine formaldehyde has been extensively employed as a functional material for conversion and energy storage due to the decent flexibility, high porosity, and low price [174]. The interrelated porous conductive carbon foam (CF) networks can be produced by directly heating MF. Wang et al. [133] designed and synthesized a 3D interconnected architecture comprising cobalt nanoparticle and dispersed \(\mathrm{Co-N}_{x}\) species co-doping CNT arrays immobilized on the CF (Co-NCNT@CF, Fig. 5(j)) via a facile pyrolysis strategy. Such a sponge architecture acts as not only a 3D interconnected conductive carbon network but also a host scaffold for a high S-containing electrode. Furthermore, the multifunctional \(\mathrm{Co-N}_{x}\) species uniformly dispersed into CNT arrays could efficiently trap and manipulate catalytical conversion of PSs, as displayed in Fig. 5(k). Notably, even at a high S loading of \(7.0~\mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\) and an electrolyte/sulfur ratio of \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\), this cell achieved a high areal capacity of \(4.34~\mathrm{mAh\cdot cm^{-2}}\) at \(0.1~\mathrm{C}\) within 100 cycles.
Beyond the materials previously mentioned, some metal oxides exhibit strong chemisorption to PSs species due to their strong polarity, which leads to excellent cycling durability. For example, Liu et al. [42] synthesized ultrathin \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) nanosheets rooted in \(\mathrm{Co/N}\)-doping porous carbon nano-box through thermally annealing the ZIF-67 (Co MOF) precursor and subsequently subjecting it to a solvothermal reaction with \(\mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\). Consequently, as expected, this electrode delivered exceptional rate performance even at \(10~\mathrm{C}\), which is better than most previously published MOF-derived C-related S host materials. The remarkable electrochemical performances should be attributed to the \(\mathrm{Co/N}\) co-doped carbon framework's favourable surface structure and high conductivity and the strong charge coupling with \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) nanosheets.
Despite the improvements seen in pristine MOFs as sulfur cathodes for LSBs, their poor electrical conductivities, specific surface areas, and particle sizes may cause issues such as sluggish
redox dynamics and limited sulfur utilization, leading to unsatisfactory electrochemical performance, including poor rate capability and low cyclic stability. This could greatly limit the practical utilization of MOFs in LSBs. To address this issue, the combination of pristine MOFs with conductive materials, including carbonaceous materials and polymers, has shown some promises. However, the lack of variety of the MOFs composites must be taken into consideration. MOF derivatives and their composites have also shown advantages, such as enhanced conductivity and electrochemical properties for LSBs [137, 138]. Additionally, factors such as tap density, sulfur content, and areal sulfur loading in the cathode must be investigated and optimized to attain high energy density in LSBs. For many MOFs derivatives, low tap density may not only result in an inadequate volumetric energy density but also a high cathode thickness to satisfy the required specific loading, further leading to restricted diffusion pathway and low sulfur utilization. To increase the sulfur content, the sulfur host material must be exactly devised based on morphology and porosity, whereas inactive material must be taken into consideration. Suitable cathode architecture is critical for achieving a high areal sulfur loading [175]. Therefore, it is an efficient strategy to rationally design 3D MOF derivative composites with a crosslinked structure and emerging MOF composite's ion sieve for free-standing sulfur cathodes.
## 4 Advanced in-situ/operando characterization for Li-S batteries
In recent years, some advanced in-situ/operando characterization techniques have been employed to trace the interior electrochemical process and investigate these reaction mechanisms of LSBs. These techniques include UV–vis spectroscopy, Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction (XRD), X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS), and Raman spectroscopy [176, 177]. It should be noted that the term "in-situ" refers to monitoring LSBs on-site without disassembling the cell or requiring post-treatment for analysis, while "operando" refers to collecting corresponding information during cell operation without interruption. In the following, diverse in-situ/operando characterizations used for LSBs will be summarized and discussed in detail.
UV–vis spectroscopy is a powerful tool to investigate solution-based electrochemical reactions. UV absorption spectrum is formed by the transition of valence electrons from low to high energy levels after a sample absorbs UV light, resulting in absorption peaks [178]. For in-situ testing, the glass was used as window for transmitting UV light and all mesh was used as collector for cells. The LiPSs could be measured by UV absorption spectrum, making UV–vis spectroscopy a commonly used technique to analyze qualitatively and quantitatively these species in LSBs. In a study by Xu et al. [179], in-situ UV–vis spectroscopy was employed to explore the interaction between UV–vis radiation and LiPSs (see Fig. 6(a)). The results proved that only short-chain lithium sulfides showed up while long-chain PSs did not appear during the whole charging/discharging process, indicating that the sulfhydryl-functionalized cathode had an inhibiting effect on the shuttling effect (see Figs. 6(b)–6(d)). In addition, DFT calculations verified that as the massive insertion of lithium ions, the final produced PSs were short-chain LiPSs, including \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}\), which are consistent with the results obtained from the in-situ UV–vis tests.
FT-IR spectroscopy is a well-established spectroscopic technique for monitoring LSBs, which offers the advantages of low price and easy operation during electrochemical evaluations. FTIR spectroscopy is used as identifying the specific functional
groups which reflect the structure and composition of molecular according to the specific absorption of an infrared beam for different ones [187]. For in-situ FT-IR measurement, the diamond crystal was used as testing window. Furthermore, there is a hole with \(2\ \mathrm{mm}\) in the center of polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) disc and Cu foil disc, which could be used for testing LiPSs absorbed on the surface of separator during charging/discharging process, as shown in Fig. 6(e) [180]. In addition, FT-IR spectroscopy has been successfully utilized to investigate these evolutions of both PSs and triflate \(\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\right)\) among coordination states in LSBs [177].
In particular, Rafie et al. [181] used in operando FT-IR spectroscopy (see Fig. 6(f) to monitor the S–S bonds stretching vibrations at different voltages upon LSB cycling of sulfur-rich copolymers S80DIB20. Figure 6(g) displays in operando FT-IR spectrum of the S80DIB20 copolymer in the region between 460 and \(530\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). Within the discharging process, a new peak at around \(\sim\ 506\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) appeared, which was assigned to the formations of higher order LiPSs, and the broad peak shifted toward lower wavenumbers during the further discharging. Furthermore, in-situ IR results revealed that upon discharging,
long-chain and intermediate-chain PSs were present in S80DIB20, whereas these PSs were absent in the lower-weight-percent copolymer, indicating that longer sulfur bridge length resulted in the formations of soluble long-chain polysulfide and caused shuttle. On the contrary, the shorter sulfur bridge length of the low-weight-percent copolymer could not form soluble PSs. Consequently, such copolymers directly yielded solid \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) without intermediate PSs, which led to a lack of redox peaks at around \(2.3\,\mathrm{V}\) and no PSs signal in in-situ IR spectrum.
Raman spectroscopy is considered to be one of the most commonly used optical spectroscopic techniques and a useful analytical tool because of the high spatial and temporal resolution capability. The different molecules or crystals correspond to different characteristic Raman spectra, which provide the information of bond, structure, or symmetry of materials. Therefore, the structure of materials and chemical reaction could be measured through Raman technique. For in-situ Raman measurement, the cell for testing is almost the same as that of in-situ FT-IR test (shown in Fig. 6(h)). The LiPSs on surface of separator could be measured through the cell of in-situ testing [182, 188]. Moreover, owing to the strong Raman activity of intermediate such as LiPSs and the electrolyte, in-situ/operando Raman spectroscopy is highly compatible with LSBs and capable of semi-quantitatively or qualitatively monitoring the lithium PSs dissolved into the ether-based electrolytes upon cycles [176]. Additionally, the changes in LSBs within the cycling could be reflected by variations in the molecular vibrational modes of cathodes, electrolytes, SBI, and so on, which is significant for investigators to clearly explain reaction mechanisms and guide the optimization of LSBs [176, 177]. Figure 6(h) shows the in-situ Raman setup [182], Chen et al. [183] combined in operando Raman spectroscopy with DFT method to understand the complex sulfur oxidation–reduction reactions in the interface of electrolyte/C. Analysis of the in operando Raman results (see Figs. 6(i)–6(l)) and DFT calculations confirmed that the nitrogen-doping C matrix could improve the adsorption energy of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}\) \((n=4{-}8)\) and facilitate the further oxidation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{n}\) into \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\). Zhu's group [189] investigated the electrochemical transformation of the sulfur cathode in all-solid-state lithium-sulfur batteries (ASLSBs) for the first time and revealed the reaction mechanisms and kinetics through the combination of operando Raman spectroscopy and ex-situ XAS. They developed a special cell that could operate under high-pressure conditions and record the chemical evolution of sulfur in real-time. Furthermore, ex-situ XAS was used to accurately validate the chemical composition of the sulfur cathodes at different depths of discharging and state of charging. The results successfully proved the exclusion of PSs in ASLSBs and the presence of intermediate \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\), which pioneers the study of the fundamental reaction mechanisms of sulfur in ASLSBs and offers a thread for designing high-performance XAS.
XAS is an effective tool that offers element-specific information and has been widely employed to investigate the structure change in crystal and amorphous electrode material, including various PSs in LSBs, which helps to understand the charging/discharging mechanism [190, 191]. XAS could be an element probe for testing the electronic structure of materials due to induced electronic excitations from X-ray photon. The XAS energy regions include two parts: One is X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) which reflects the information of electronic structure and oxidation state of the element; and second is extend X-ray absorption fine structure (XANES) which reflects the local structure information such as bond instance and coordination number. The circular X-ray penetration window, located on the cathode disk, is composed of a polyimide film that facilitates the
detection of LiPSs on the surface of the separator [192]. As shown in Figs. 6(m)–6(p), Ling et al. [184] and Zhang et al. [185] used in-situ and operando XAS techniques to study the charge redox reaction mechanisms of nanosphere \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) particle electrodes in real-time. During the initial charging process, \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) was directly transformed into S element through the two-phase conversion without the formations of polysulfide. However, PSs were found at the beginning of the second charging process, which was related with the residual polysulfide at the end of the initial discharging cycle, and this finding offers a novel basic understanding of the charge mechanisms of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) electrodes.
XRD is a widely used technique to characterize the chemical and physical property of crystalline electrode material in various batteries. That is because the different materials or different crystallinity of structured materials could produce the specific diffraction patterns. The cell for in-situ XRD measurement consists of Be of Kapton film window, which could be possible for X-ray penetration [193, 194]. In the context of LSBs, XRD is a powerful tool to study the dissolutions and formations of crystal \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and its corresponding final product of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) at different charging states. More importantly, the behaviors of \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) strongly depend on the selection of the electrolyte system and current density. Some researchers [50, 186] used in-situ XRD in testing of the FeCoNi PBA-S-PPy cathode to uncover the polysulfide redox reaction mechanism and kinetics upon the discharge/charge cycling (see Figs. 6(q) and 6(r)). The in-situ XRD scans (see Fig. 6(s)) showed that the peak of crystalline S8 at \(23.3^{\circ}\) progressively weakened and eventually disappeared during the discharging process, indicating that S reacted with \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)to generate \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). Meanwhile, the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) peak at \(27.2^{\circ}\) appeared, strengthened, and reached the maximum at the final of discharging. During the subsequent charge cycle, the peak intensity of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) gradually decreased, and simultaneously the sulfur peak reappeared, indicating that the FeCoNi PBA-S-PPy cathode exhibited excellent electrochemical reversibility.
Overall, due to their simplicity, non-destructiveness, and high temporal and spatial resolution, in-situoperando UV–vis, FT-IR, and Raman techniques have been acquired significant attentions and extensively utilized to track various changes occurring in the LSBs system during the discharge/charge process. In-situ UV–vis spectroscopy could efficiently detect the soluble polysulfide, which often displays wide absorption band in the range from 185 to \(1000\ \mathrm{nm}\) . In contrast, in-situ FT-IR spectroscopy can determine individual Li polysulfide species according to the S–S vibrating modes located in a narrow range of 470 to \(510~\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [180]. However, the generally low solubility of \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in the commonly used electrolyte poses a limitation on the concentration of these species, and both in-situ UV–vis and FT-IR characterizations are unable to capture phase change in the solid side. Conversely, in-situ/operando Raman spectroscopy can effectively probe the signal of the polysulfide from both the electrolytes and solid sides simultaneously. Nevertheless, insitu/operando Raman spectroscopy is not appropriate to quantitatively analyze different polysulfide species because some of their Raman peaks overlap, and some polysulfide intermediates might result in fluorescence emission, which could mask important peak information [176]. In-situ/operando XRD is an effective tool for recording the real-time phase changes of crystalline materials, such as LiPSs in the LSBs system. In-situ/operando XAS at the S K-edge can monitor the formation of LiPSs during the cycling of LSBs. However, the use of insitu/operando XRD and XAS requires advanced equipment with a complicated cell devise, which should be inconvenient for researchers [186, 195]. Based on the above discussion, using only the in-situ/operando characterization technique is challenging in
clarifying the accurate redox mechanisms upon the discharging/charging process in LSBs. Therefore, the combination of two or even more in-situ/operando characterization techniques is necessary to increase the probability of success in investigating the intermediate discharge and charging products during the cycling of LSBs.
## 5 Conclusions and perspectives
In recent decades, MOF-related materials, including pristine MOFs, MOF composite, MOF derivative, and MOF derivative composites, have attracted much interest as emerging research topics in energy storage devices, particularly in the field of LSBs. This is due to the unique feature of MOFs including specific surface areas and high porosity, adjustable pore structures, controllable morphologies/structures, active metal sites, and tunable chemical compositions that could be utilized to modify and improve the electrochemical performance of the sulfur cathode material. As a result, as illustrated in Fig. 7, significant progresses have been made on the development of MOF-related materials as advanced sulfur cathode materials in LSBs. However, the applications of pristine MOFs as sulfur hosts are limited by their weak structural stability and poor electrical conductivity. And great majority of pristine MOFs undergo structural collapse, particularly under water/moisture, acid, or basic environment, resulting in weak cyclic stability upon the cycling process. Correspondingly, MOF derivatives with improved conductivity and better stability, such as MOF-derived carbonaceous materials, metal compound, and their composite, are considered promising sulfur cathode materials for LSBs. It is worth noting that pristine MOFs and their derivatives with high porous structures and large specific surface areas would cause low CEs and low tap density. Additionally, MOFs-based cathodes also have good prospects for application in all-solid-state batteries. However, their viability is significantly constrained by issues related to physical contact, space-charge layer, and the location/number of active sites [91]. As such, the ideal MOFs candidates should possess the following characteristics: (1) sufficient electronic conductivity and suitable ionic pore size, ensuring effective contact with other components; (2) moderate bond energy and robust frameworks to maintain stable ion pathways during the cycling process; and (3) the active sites can be further increased through the addition of extra cathode active materials or solid-state electrolytes [196, 197].
Second, to our best knowledge, the primary impediment to the industrial production and commercial applications of MOFs lies in the synthesis strategies. Conventional solvothermal methods for MOFs suffer from drawbacks including harsh synthesis conditions, prolonged reaction periods, and difficulty in continuous synthesis [198, 199]. Emerging synthesis methodologies encompass microwave-assisted, electrochemical, mechanochemical, sono-chemical, spray drying, hot pressing, and continuous flow techniques. Notably, the hot pressing, spray drying, and continuous flow synthesis methods stand out due to their simplicity and rapidity. These continuous approaches hold promise for large-scale batch production, demonstrating suitability for industrial applications. Moreover, concerning the commercial implementation of MOFs, careful attention must be given to the expenses associated with reaction precursor components, including metal ions, organic ligands, and solvents. Opting for solvent-free synthesis methods holds potential for enhanced cost-effectiveness and environmental sustainability [200, 201].
Lastly, the development of high-throughput computational screening (HTCS) to construct large materials databases is at the heart of the Materials Genome Initiative (MGI) Project. As mentioned in Fig. 7, HTCS stands as an influential tool to explore the quantitative structure–property relationship to facilitate the rational design of top-performing MOFs [202, 203]. The MOFs databases employed in HTCS primarily consist of two main categories: experimentally synthesized MOFs, exceeding 69,666 entries, archived within the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC); and hypothetical MOFs, surpassing 130,000 in quantity, created using computer-aided tools through computational processes involving bottom-up or top-down approaches. The primary screening strategies comprise molecular simulation and machine learning-based HTCS. Molecular simulations, notably involving techniques like grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations, constitute the predominant method for conducting the vast majority of HTCS analyses [204]. The machine learning approach operates by utilizing a limited set of structural features from MOFs and their performance data as a "training set". Through diverse algorithms, it constructs a "knowledge base", which essentially represents a mathematical model capable of accurately describing the correlation between the structural characteristics of an MOF and its performance. Subsequently, this resulting model is employed to predict the performance of
numerous MOFs in a "test set" with unknown performance. This approach significantly reduces computation time while enhancing screening efficiency. Commonly employed machine learning methodologies at present encompass multilinear regression (MLR), decision tree (DT), K-nearest neighbor (KNN), support vector machines (SVMs), artificial neural networks (ANN), random forest (RF), and genetic algorithm (GA) [205, 206]. Overall, the fusion of HTCS with big data methods such as machine learning/artificial intelligence (AI) algorithm has heralded a new era in material computation. Notably, these integrated approaches possess the capacity to intelligently refine emerging computational models, circumvent inherent cognitive biases in human cognition, expand the scope of variable exploration, and generate fresh insights into the characteristics of target materials. Despite the limitations, research on MOF-related materials as advanced sulfur cathode materials for LSBs is still in its infancy. Therefore, the following points should be considered in the future development of MOF-related materials in LSBs.
(1) Firstly, the ideal sulfur cathodes should own several basic characteristics, such as large specific surface areas, high porosity, easy to access active sites, excellent mass transport and electron transfer capability, and lasting stability.
(2) Up to date, only a few typical MOFs are selected as sulfur hosts. Here, novel organic frameworks materials with good conductivity and high chemical and thermal stability need to be developed to synthesize advanced host materials. Data-driven machine learning technique as well as artificial intelligence-assisted high-throughput screening will accelerate discovery and design of target MOFs matrix.
(3) Grafting the desired atom/group or introducing structural stabilizer within the MOFs is regarded as the functionalized strategy to enhance the conductivity or stability of pristine MOFs. For them, the major challenge is to exploit the effective functional component, scalable and facile preparation procedure, and low cost of synthesis.
(4) Apart from the gravimetric/volumetric energy density, rate performance, and cyclability, some other critical parameters, such as sulfur loading, tap density, and cost, should be taken into consideration, and a balance between them should be established to pursue the practical sulfur cathode materials and approach the commercialization of LSB storage systems.
(5) It is indispensable to explore the fundamental mechanisms of MOF-based sulfur cathode materials in LSBs both experimentally and theoretically. While the basic reactions and relevant products upon the charging/discharging process have been revealed, the transportation and transformation of electroactive components in LSBs are not yet fully understood. Additionally, the influence of MOF-based materials on the dissolution/diffusion of polysulfide must be closely examined to develop high-energy and long-lasting LSBs.
(6) The relationship between the structure/compositions and relevant electrochemical property remains to be clarified and explored in future research. Apart from real-time monitoring experimental methods, theoretical techniques including DFT calculation and molecular dynamics simulations are necessary to acquire quantized guidance on MOF-based materials for advanced LSBs.
(7) Finally, MOFs-related materials suffer from issues such as the high cost of organic linkers, cumbersome manufacturing processes, and low productivity, which significantly hinder their practical applications. Therefore, to achieve the industrial applications of MOFs-related sulfur cathode materials, more research efforts should be directed towards developing practical, environment-friendly, and low-price technologies for the synthesis of MOFs-related materials.
Overall, MOF-related materials are emerging as promising sulfur cathode materials for LSBs. Despite significant progress made in recent years, there still exist challenges to overcome for the commercialization of LSBs. It will take time and effort to achieve practical application of MOF-related materials in LSBs. We believe and expect that this review could offer the useful guidance for the future development of MOF-related materials for LSBs and other energy storage devices.
